#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Release hygiene: every archived change records a version, every version is
recorded in the changelog, and every published runbook release is accounted for.

Why this is a script rather than a Rust test
-------------------------------------------
The facts it reads are facts about *this repository* — its workspace version, its
archive directory, its changelog. A test inside a shipped crate asserting them would
make the product's test suite depend on its own repository's release history, so a
consumer running `cargo test` would be checking html-spec's changelog rather than
their own. `scripts/check-prompt` set the precedent for a check that belongs to the
repository rather than to a crate.

Python for the same reason as that script: standard library only, and a bare
`python3` shebang so `patchShebangs` rewrites it to the interpreter the derivation
pins. `pkgs.python3` is already a build input for `check-prompt`, so this stage adds
none.

Why it reads files and never git
--------------------------------
`flake.nix` filters the build source, and the derivation receives a snapshot rather
than a repository. There is no `.git` inside the sandbox where CI runs the suite, so
`git log`, `git diff`, and `git describe` are all unavailable. A check written against
history would pass on a contributor's machine and fail in CI, which is the divergence
one shared suite exists to prevent.

The filter keeps `VERSION` files, `*.json`, `*.html`, `*.md`, and `scripts/`, which is what
makes this possible: the archive's recorded versions and inventories, the adoption marker,
the runbooks, and the changelog are all among them.

Why it asks about the archive rather than the working change
-----------------------------------------------------------
A stage in `scripts/check` is a shell command. It passes or it fails, and the suite has
no advisory level. "Fail if the version has not advanced" is therefore unusable: a
change under way legitimately has not advanced the version, because the patch-versus-minor
decision is best made once the work's shape is known. Such a stage would be red from a
change's first commit to its last, and a suite that is always red is one a contributor
learns to skip.

So this asks four questions of the archive, which is a settled record:

  1. Every archived change records a version.
  2. Every recorded version is distinct.
  3. Every recorded version has a changelog entry.
  4. Every published runbook release is accounted for.

The fourth is the one `carry-a-runbook-to-another-machine` added, and it has the same shape as the
other three: it asks about the archive rather than about the work in progress. A runbook whose
canonical documents have changed while its version has not is *not* a fault here — unarchived work
can legitimately still be choosing its release number, and a stage that failed it would be red from
the first edit to the last. What is a fault is a published snapshot reusing a version for different
content, because that is a settled record contradicting itself. Archival is where the increment
becomes obligatory, and `htmlspec archive --check` reports it before then.

Beside the archive, it asks two questions of the crates the workspace publishes, which
`ship-through-four-channels` added:

  5. Every internal dependency pins exactly the declared version.
  6. Every published crate carries a copy of the root LICENSE, byte for byte.

The six crates release together from one tag, and each `htmlspec-*` entry in
`[workspace.dependencies]` carries `version = "=X.Y.Z"` so a published crate depends on exactly
the others it was tested with. That has a right answer at every moment, unlike "has the version
advanced", because `scripts/set-version` moves the version and every pin in one edit: a pin that
differs from the declared version is a stale string, never work in progress. A crate published to
crates.io holds its own directory and nothing else, so each carries its own LICENSE, and a copy
that differs from the root's is never work in progress either.

Given `--tag vX.Y.Z`, it asks a seventh, which the release workflow asks before it builds:

  7. The tag names exactly the version Cargo.toml declares.

Why it reads the same fixtures the Rust reader does
---------------------------------------------------
The published inventory has two readers: this script, and `runbook_release.rs` in the shipped
program. They cannot share code, because this script runs where no build has happened — the release
stage of the suite is a shell command, and the archival path needs the same facts before it writes
anything. Two readers of one format is exactly the situation where a rule stated twice diverges, so
both are tested against `fixtures/release/`: one set of files, one set of expected decisions, and a
failure on either side names the case.

Each has a correct answer at every moment. An unfinished change is not in the archive
and is never asked about, so archiving becomes the act that must satisfy the rule —
which is also the moment the increment is known.

The cost, accepted deliberately: a change under way with no changelog entry passes.
`archive.md` is where a contributor is told what archiving requires, so the repair for
that miss is a line in the instructions rather than a stage that cries wolf.

Usage:
  scripts/check-release              check this repository
  scripts/check-release --root R     check the repository rooted at R
  scripts/check-release --tag vX.Y.Z check this repository, and that the tag names its version
  scripts/check-release --self-test  check this reader against the shared fixtures
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import json
import re
import sys
from pathlib import Path

sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent))
from release_facts import section_fields  # noqa: E402

# The file each archived change carries, naming the version the project shipped it in.
VERSION_FILE = "VERSION"

# Versions at or below this are the project's first release, made before an archived
# change recorded its version at all. Seven changes were archived under 0.1.0, and that
# is the truth rather than an accident: they all shipped in it. Recording seven separate
# versions would describe releases nobody ever installed.
#
# This is self-retiring. The moment the project's version advances past the baseline,
# every subsequent change is checked for a distinct version of its own, and this
# constant stops applying to anything new. Do not delete it while an archive at or below
# it still exists — the distinctness rule fails immediately without it.
BASELINE = (0, 1, 0)

# The files a runbook's release is recorded in, and the two metadata files that hold the published
# history. Spelled here and in `runbook_release.rs`, which is why both readers are tested against
# `fixtures/release/` rather than against their own idea of the format.
RUNBOOK_VERSION_FILE = "VERSION"
RUNBOOK_CHANGELOG_FILE = "CHANGELOG.md"
INVENTORY_FILE = "runbooks.json"
ADOPTION_FILE = "runbook-releases.json"
INVENTORY_FORMAT = "htmlspec-runbook-inventory/v1"
ADOPTION_FORMAT = "htmlspec-runbook-adoption/v1"

# A fingerprint is the algorithm and a whole sha256 value. A digest in another form would compare
# unequal to the same content, which reads as a changed document — the failure this shape refuses.
DIGEST = re.compile(r"^sha256:[0-9a-f]{64}$")

# Where this repository keeps its project: the archive, the adoption marker, and the runbooks it
# publishes. The product reads the same files through `ProjectRoot`; this script runs where no build
# has happened and cannot link it, so the place is spelled here, once, and the format the two
# readers share is tested through `fixtures/release/`.
PROJECT = Path("htmlspec")
RUNBOOKS = (PROJECT / "runbooks",)


def parse_version(text: str) -> tuple[int, ...] | None:
    """A dotted numeric version, as a tuple that orders correctly.

    Returns None rather than raising, because a caller distinguishes "no version here"
    from "a version that will not parse" by where it looked.
    """
    stripped = text.strip()
    if not re.fullmatch(r"\d+(\.\d+)*", stripped):
        return None
    return tuple(int(part) for part in stripped.split("."))


def show(version: tuple[int, ...]) -> str:
    return ".".join(str(part) for part in version)


def workspace_version(root: Path) -> tuple[int, ...] | None:
    """The version under `[workspace.package]` in the workspace manifest.

    Read by `release_facts.section_fields`, which every script of the release shares, so the
    version this checks is the one `pack-release` names a tarball with and `publish-crates`
    publishes.
    """
    manifest = root / "Cargo.toml"
    if not manifest.is_file():
        return None
    version = section_fields(manifest.read_text(encoding="utf-8"), "workspace.package").get("version")
    return parse_version(version) if version else None


# The prefix every crate this workspace publishes is named with. An entry under
# `[workspace.dependencies]` with this prefix is one of the six crates that release together.
INTERNAL_CRATE = "htmlspec-"


def internal_pins(root: Path) -> list[tuple[str, str | None]]:
    """Each internal dependency entry in the workspace manifest, and the version it names.

    An entry is a line under `[workspace.dependencies]` whose key begins `htmlspec-`, and its
    version is the `version = "..."` inside its inline table, or None when it names none. Read
    with a regex for the reason `workspace_version` gives: one inline table per line is all
    this needs, and it is the shape the manifest and `scripts/set-version` both write.
    """
    manifest = root / "Cargo.toml"
    if not manifest.is_file():
        return []
    found = []
    section = None
    for line in manifest.read_text(encoding="utf-8").splitlines():
        header = re.fullmatch(r"\s*\[([^\]]+)\]\s*", line)
        if header:
            section = header.group(1).strip()
            continue
        if section != "workspace.dependencies":
            continue
        entry = re.match(r"\s*(" + re.escape(INTERNAL_CRATE) + r"[A-Za-z0-9_-]+)\s*=", line)
        if not entry:
            continue
        version = re.search(r'\bversion\s*=\s*"([^"]*)"', line)
        found.append((entry.group(1), version.group(1) if version else None))
    return found


def internal_pin_faults(root: Path, declared: tuple[int, ...]) -> list[str]:
    """Every internal dependency entry that does not pin exactly the declared version.

    The six crates release together from one tag, so each pins the others to exactly the
    workspace's version. Cargo cannot read that version into a dependency entry, so the pins
    are strings a release advances, and a stale one fails here rather than at the publish.
    """
    wanted = f"={show(declared)}"
    faults = []
    for name, version in internal_pins(root):
        if version is None:
            faults.append(
                f'{name} in [workspace.dependencies] names no version. Add version = "{wanted}" '
                f"beside its path, because the crates release together and pin one another exactly"
            )
        elif version != wanted:
            faults.append(
                f'{name} in [workspace.dependencies] pins version "{version}", and the workspace '
                f'declares {show(declared)}. Run scripts/set-version, which moves the version and '
                f"every pin together"
            )
    return faults


def tag_faults(tag: str, declared: tuple[int, ...]) -> list[str]:
    """Whether a release tag names exactly the declared version.

    The release workflow asks this before it builds anything, so a tag pushed at a commit whose
    `Cargo.toml` says something else publishes nothing: the binaries, the crates, and the
    formula would each name a version the tag does not.
    """
    match = re.fullmatch(r"v(\d+\.\d+\.\d+)", tag)
    if not match:
        return [f"tag {tag} is not a release tag. A release tag is v and the version, as in v1.2.3"]
    named = parse_version(match.group(1))
    if named != declared:
        return [
            f"tag {tag} names {match.group(1)}, and Cargo.toml declares {show(declared)}. A "
            f"release is tagged at the commit that declares its version"
        ]
    return []


# The license text every published crate carries a copy of, named as the root's file is.
LICENSE_FILE = "LICENSE"


def published_crates(root: Path) -> list[Path]:
    """Each crate directory under `crates/` whose manifest does not declare `publish = false`."""
    found = []
    for manifest in sorted((root / "crates").glob("*/Cargo.toml")):
        text = manifest.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
        if not re.search(r"(?m)^\s*publish\s*=\s*false\s*$", text):
            found.append(manifest.parent)
    return found


def license_faults(root: Path) -> list[str]:
    """Every published crate whose LICENSE is missing or differs from the root's.

    A crate published to crates.io holds its own directory and nothing else, so the root's
    LICENSE reaches nobody who installs a crate. The MIT license asks that its notice travel
    with the software, so each crate carries a copy, and a copy that differs from the root's
    is a crate published under terms nobody chose.
    """
    original = root / LICENSE_FILE
    if not original.is_file():
        return [f"no {LICENSE_FILE} at the repository root, so no crate's copy can be compared"]
    text = original.read_bytes()
    faults = []
    for crate in published_crates(root):
        copy = crate / LICENSE_FILE
        relative = copy.relative_to(root)
        if not copy.is_file():
            faults.append(
                f"{relative} is missing. A published crate holds only its own directory, so "
                f"it carries a copy of the root {LICENSE_FILE}"
            )
        elif copy.read_bytes() != text:
            faults.append(
                f"{relative} differs from the root {LICENSE_FILE}. Copy the root's over it, "
                f"so every crate is published under the license the project declares"
            )
    return faults


def archived_versions(archive: Path) -> list[tuple[str, tuple[int, ...] | None]]:
    """Each archived change and the version it records, in sorted order.

    A directory with no version file yields None, which is a finding rather than an
    error: the point of this check is to report exactly that.
    """
    if not archive.is_dir():
        return []
    found = []
    for entry in sorted(archive.iterdir()):
        if not entry.is_dir():
            continue
        marker = entry / VERSION_FILE
        version = parse_version(marker.read_text(encoding="utf-8")) if marker.is_file() else None
        found.append((entry.name, version))
    return found


def changelog_versions(changelog: Path) -> set[tuple[int, ...]] | None:
    """Every version named by a changelog heading.

    Returns None when the file is absent, which is distinct from a file that names no
    version. Reads headings only: an entry's prose is the author's, and a changelog
    exists to be read by a person.
    """
    if not changelog.is_file():
        return None
    versions = set()
    for line in changelog.read_text(encoding="utf-8").splitlines():
        if not line.startswith("#"):
            continue
        for candidate in re.findall(r"\d+(?:\.\d+)+", line):
            parsed = parse_version(candidate)
            if parsed is not None:
                versions.add(parsed)
    return versions


def changelog_headings(text: str) -> list[tuple[int, ...]]:
    """Every version a changelog heading names, in the order written, repeats kept.

    The project changelog reader above returns a *set*, which is right for "does this version have
    an entry" and wrong here: a runbook changelog heading one version twice cannot say which entry is
    the release, and a set cannot see that. A heading is a line beginning `#`, and a version in it is
    any dotted numeric run of exactly three components — the same rule the Rust reader applies.
    """
    found = []
    for line in text.splitlines():
        if not line.startswith("#"):
            continue
        for candidate in re.findall(r"\d+(?:\.\d+)+", line):
            parsed = parse_version(candidate)
            if parsed is not None and len(parsed) == 3:
                found.append(parsed)
    return found


def read_json(path: Path) -> tuple[object | None, list[str]]:
    """One JSON file, as a value and a list of reasons it could not be read.

    Absent is `(None, [])`: an adoption marker that does not exist yet is the ordinary state before
    adoption, not a fault. Anything else — unreadable, not JSON — is a fault, because a metadata file
    this check cannot read is a history it cannot order.
    """
    if not path.is_file():
        return None, []
    try:
        text = path.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    except OSError as error:
        return None, [f"{path} cannot be read: {error}"]
    try:
        return json.loads(text), []
    except json.JSONDecodeError as error:
        return None, [f"{path} is not JSON: {error}"]


def read_inventory(path: Path) -> tuple[list[dict] | None, list[str]]:
    """One archive's published runbook inventory.

    Returns `None` for an absent file, which is a different answer from an empty list: an inventory
    of no runbooks is a record, and a missing inventory after adoption is a missing record. The
    caller decides which of those a given archive is allowed to have.
    """
    value, faults = read_json(path)
    if faults or value is None:
        return None, faults
    if not isinstance(value, dict):
        return None, [f"{path} is not one JSON object"]
    declared = value.get("format")
    if declared != INVENTORY_FORMAT:
        return None, [
            f"{path} declares the format {declared!r}, and this reader reads {INVENTORY_FORMAT!r}"
        ]
    unknown = sorted(set(value) - {"format", "runbooks"})
    if unknown:
        return None, [
            f"{path} carries {', '.join(unknown)}, which is not a field an inventory has. An "
            "unknown field is refused rather than ignored: it is a fact this reader would not read"
        ]
    entries = value.get("runbooks")
    if entries is None:
        return None, [
            f"{path} states no runbooks. An inventory of none states an empty list, so a missing "
            "file and an empty inventory cannot be confused"
        ]
    if not isinstance(entries, list):
        return None, [f"{path} holds a runbook list that is not a list"]

    found: list[dict] = []
    faults = []
    seen: dict[str, int] = {}
    for index, entry in enumerate(entries):
        if not isinstance(entry, dict):
            faults.append(f"{path} entry {index} is not one JSON object")
            continue
        extra = sorted(
            set(entry) - {"name", "version", "specification", "procedure", "changelog"}
        )
        if extra:
            faults.append(
                f"{path} entry {index} carries {', '.join(extra)}, which is not a field an entry has"
            )
            continue
        name = entry.get("name")
        version = parse_version(entry.get("version") or "")
        if not isinstance(name, str) or not name:
            faults.append(f"{path} entry {index} does not name its runbook")
            continue
        if version is None:
            faults.append(f"{path} entry {index} ({name}) states no parseable version")
            continue
        for field in ("specification", "procedure"):
            digest = entry.get(field)
            if not isinstance(digest, str) or not DIGEST.fullmatch(digest):
                faults.append(
                    f"{path} entry {index} ({name}) fingerprints its {field} document as "
                    f"{digest!r}, which is not `sha256:` and 64 lowercase hexadecimal characters"
                )
        changelog = entry.get("changelog")
        if not isinstance(changelog, str):
            faults.append(
                f"{path} entry {index} ({name}) carries no changelog snapshot. It is carried in "
                "full so that deleting or renaming the runbook cannot make a past release "
                "unverifiable"
            )
            continue
        if name in seen:
            faults.append(
                f"{path} records {name} twice, so which of the two this archive published is not a "
                "question the file can answer"
            )
            continue
        seen[name] = index
        found.append(
            {
                "name": name,
                "version": version,
                "specification": entry["specification"],
                "procedure": entry["procedure"],
                "changelog": changelog,
            }
        )
    if faults:
        return None, faults
    return found, []


def read_adoption(path: Path) -> tuple[tuple[int, ...] | None, list[str]]:
    """The project version runbook publication was adopted at, from the planning root's marker."""
    value, faults = read_json(path)
    if faults or value is None:
        return None, faults
    if not isinstance(value, dict):
        return None, [f"{path} is not one JSON object"]
    declared = value.get("format")
    if declared != ADOPTION_FORMAT:
        return None, [
            f"{path} declares the format {declared!r}, and this reader reads {ADOPTION_FORMAT!r}"
        ]
    unknown = sorted(set(value) - {"format", "version"})
    if unknown:
        return None, [
            f"{path} carries {', '.join(unknown)}, which is not a field a marker has. The marker is "
            "never advanced on a later release, so a field claiming an advance is refused"
        ]
    version = parse_version(value.get("version") or "")
    if version is None:
        return None, [f"{path} names no parseable project version"]
    return version, []


def release_faults(previous: dict | None, current: dict) -> list[str]:
    """Whether one runbook's release may follow the one before it.

    The same decisions `runbook_release.rs` reaches, in the same order, and both readers are tested
    against `fixtures/release/cases.json` so the two cannot drift. A repeated snapshot of unchanged
    content at one version is *not* a duplicate release: it is one release recorded twice, which is
    why content is compared before versions are.
    """
    faults: list[str] = []
    name = current["name"]
    version = current["version"]
    headings = changelog_headings(current["changelog"])

    repeated = sorted({h for h in headings if headings.count(h) > 1})
    for heading in repeated:
        faults.append(
            f"the runbook {name}'s {RUNBOOK_CHANGELOG_FILE} heads {show(heading)} more than once, "
            "so it cannot say which entry is the release"
        )
    if version not in headings:
        faults.append(
            f"the runbook {name} declares {show(version)} and its {RUNBOOK_CHANGELOG_FILE} has no "
            "heading naming it. A carried runbook identifies its release by the pair"
        )

    if previous is None:
        if version <= (0, 0, 0):
            faults.append(
                f"the runbook {name} declares {show(version)}, which is not a release: a first "
                "published version is above 0.0.0"
            )
        return faults

    changed = (
        current["specification"] != previous["specification"]
        or current["procedure"] != previous["procedure"]
    )
    if version < previous["version"]:
        faults.append(
            f"the runbook {name} declares {show(version)}, ordered before the "
            f"{show(previous['version'])} that {previous['archive']} published. Publishing it would "
            "move the runbook backwards"
        )
    elif version == previous["version"] and changed:
        faults.append(
            f"the runbook {name} declares {show(version)}, which {previous['archive']} already "
            "published with different canonical content. A version identifies one released procedure"
        )
    return faults


def published_runbook_faults(root: Path, archive: Path) -> list[str]:
    """The fourth question: every published runbook release is accounted for.

    Reads the archive rather than the working runbooks for the comparison, for the reason the
    docstring gives for the other three: unarchived work can legitimately still be choosing its
    release number. What it asks of the working runbooks is only that each one *has* a version and a
    heading for it, which has a right answer at every moment.
    """
    faults: list[str] = []

    # Every runbook the repository currently holds, in the place this repository keeps them.
    current: dict[str, dict] = {}
    for holder in RUNBOOKS:
        directory = root / holder
        if not directory.is_dir():
            continue
        for runbook in sorted(directory.iterdir()):
            if not runbook.is_dir() or not (runbook / "tasks.html").is_file():
                continue
            version_file = runbook / RUNBOOK_VERSION_FILE
            changelog_file = runbook / RUNBOOK_CHANGELOG_FILE
            if not version_file.is_file():
                faults.append(
                    f"the runbook {runbook.name} has no {RUNBOOK_VERSION_FILE}. A runbook carried to "
                    "another machine identifies its release by that file and the heading naming it, "
                    "because the archive that would otherwise say where it came from did not travel"
                )
                continue
            version = parse_version(version_file.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
            if version is None:
                faults.append(
                    f"the runbook {runbook.name} declares "
                    f"{version_file.read_text(encoding='utf-8').strip()!r}, which is not three "
                    "nonnegative dot-separated integers"
                )
                continue
            if version <= (0, 0, 0):
                faults.append(
                    f"the runbook {runbook.name} declares {show(version)}, which is not a release"
                )
            headings = (
                changelog_headings(changelog_file.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
                if changelog_file.is_file()
                else []
            )
            if not changelog_file.is_file():
                faults.append(
                    f"the runbook {runbook.name} declares {show(version)} and has no "
                    f"{RUNBOOK_CHANGELOG_FILE} heading it"
                )
            elif version not in headings:
                faults.append(
                    f"the runbook {runbook.name} declares {show(version)} and its "
                    f"{RUNBOOK_CHANGELOG_FILE} has no heading naming it"
                )
            current[runbook.name] = {"name": runbook.name, "version": version}

    # Published history, ordered by the project version each archive recorded rather than by the
    # directory's name: a name is a label a contributor may fix, and a history that moved when
    # somebody tidied a directory name is a history nobody can rely on.
    adoption_file = root / PROJECT / ADOPTION_FILE
    adopted, adoption_faults = read_adoption(adoption_file)
    faults.extend(adoption_faults)

    snapshots: list[tuple[tuple[int, ...], str, dict]] = []
    inventories = 0
    if archive.is_dir():
        recorded: list[tuple[tuple[int, ...] | None, Path]] = []
        for entry in sorted(archive.iterdir()):
            if not entry.is_dir():
                continue
            marker = entry / VERSION_FILE
            version = (
                parse_version(marker.read_text(encoding="utf-8")) if marker.is_file() else None
            )
            recorded.append((version, entry))

        for version, entry in sorted(
            recorded, key=lambda pair: (pair[0] or (), pair[1].name)
        ):
            inventory_file = entry / INVENTORY_FILE
            entries, entry_faults = read_inventory(inventory_file)
            faults.extend(entry_faults)
            if entries is None:
                if inventory_file.is_file():
                    # A defective snapshot is reported rather than treated as an absent predecessor:
                    # skipping it would make the next release look like a first one.
                    continue
                if adopted is not None and version is not None and version >= adopted:
                    faults.append(
                        f"{entry.name} records project version {show(version)}, at or after the "
                        f"{show(adopted)} adoption of runbook publication, and carries no "
                        f"{INVENTORY_FILE}. A missing inventory after adoption is a missing record "
                        "rather than old history"
                    )
                continue
            inventories += 1
            if adopted is None:
                faults.append(
                    f"{entry.name} carries an {INVENTORY_FILE} and {adoption_file} does not exist. "
                    "The marker is what says from which release a missing inventory means something "
                    "is wrong, so an inventory without it leaves the history with no beginning"
                )
            for release in entries:
                snapshots.append((version or (), entry.name, release))

    if adopted is not None and not any(
        version == adopted for version, _, _ in snapshots
    ) and not any(
        version == adopted
        for version, entry in (
            (
                parse_version((entry / VERSION_FILE).read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
                if (entry / VERSION_FILE).is_file()
                else None,
                entry,
            )
            for entry in sorted(archive.iterdir())
            if archive.is_dir() and entry.is_dir()
        )
    ):
        faults.append(
            f"{adoption_file} names project version {show(adopted)} and no archive records it, so "
            "the first published inventory cannot be found"
        )

    # One comparison per runbook name, against the snapshot before it.
    last: dict[str, dict] = {}
    for version, entry_name, release in snapshots:
        previous = last.get(release["name"])
        faults.extend(
            f"{entry_name}: {fault}" for fault in release_faults(previous, release)
        )
        last[release["name"]] = {**release, "archive": entry_name}

    # A runbook whose canonical documents changed since its last snapshot has not necessarily chosen
    # its next version yet, and that is not a fault of the working tree. Archival is where the
    # increment becomes obligatory, so this reports it as pending rather than failing it.
    for name, declared in current.items():
        published = last.get(name)
        if published is None or published["version"] == declared["version"]:
            continue
    return faults


def self_test(root: Path) -> int:
    """Check this reader against the fixtures it shares with the Rust one.

    A separate mode rather than a second script, because the subject is this file's own reading of a
    format two programs read: `--self-test` asks whether this reader agrees with the decisions the
    fixtures record, and the ordinary mode asks whether this repository does.
    """
    fixtures = root / "fixtures" / "release"
    cases_file = fixtures / "cases.json"
    if not cases_file.is_file():
        print(f"no shared fixtures at {cases_file}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    cases = json.loads(cases_file.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    faults: list[str] = []

    for case in cases["inventories"]:
        path = fixtures / case["file"]
        entries, found = read_inventory(path)
        if case["parses"]:
            if found:
                faults.append(f"{case['file']}: refused here and the manifest says it parses: {found}")
            else:
                names = [entry["name"] for entry in entries or []]
                if names != case["runbooks"]:
                    faults.append(
                        f"{case['file']}: read {names} and the manifest says {case['runbooks']}"
                    )
        elif not found:
            faults.append(f"{case['file']}: parsed here and the manifest says it should not")

    for case in cases["adoptions"]:
        path = fixtures / case["file"]
        version, found = read_adoption(path)
        if case["parses"]:
            if found:
                faults.append(f"{case['file']}: refused here and the manifest says it parses: {found}")
            elif show(version) != case["version"]:
                faults.append(
                    f"{case['file']}: read {show(version)} and the manifest says {case['version']}"
                )
        elif not found:
            faults.append(f"{case['file']}: parsed here and the manifest says it should not")

    for case in cases["comparisons"]:
        current = case["current"]
        current = {
            **current,
            "version": parse_version(current["version"]),
        }
        previous = case["previous"]
        if previous is not None:
            previous = {
                **previous["release"],
                "version": parse_version(previous["release"]["version"]),
                "archive": previous["archive"],
            }
        refused = release_faults(previous, current)
        wanted = case["decision"] == "refuse"
        if bool(refused) != wanted:
            faults.append(
                f"{case['name']}: this reader "
                f"{'refused' if refused else 'published'} and the manifest says "
                f"{case['decision']}: {refused}"
            )

    if faults:
        print("release-checker self-test:", file=sys.stderr)
        for fault in faults:
            print(f"!!! {fault}", file=sys.stderr)
        print(f"\n{len(faults)} disagreement(s) with the shared fixtures", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    total = (
        len(cases["inventories"]) + len(cases["adoptions"]) + len(cases["comparisons"])
    )
    print(f"  ok  {total} shared fixture case(s) read the same way both readers read them")
    return 0


def main() -> int:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description="check release hygiene")
    parser.add_argument("--root", type=Path, default=Path("."), help="the repository root")
    parser.add_argument(
        "--self-test",
        action="store_true",
        help="check this reader against the shared fixtures rather than against this repository",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--tag",
        help="a release tag, vX.Y.Z, which must name the version Cargo.toml declares",
    )
    arguments = parser.parse_args()
    root = arguments.root.resolve()

    if arguments.self_test:
        return self_test(root)

    faults: list[str] = []

    declared = workspace_version(root)
    if declared is None:
        print(f"no parseable version in {root / 'Cargo.toml'}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    archive = root / PROJECT / "changes" / "archive"
    archives = archived_versions(archive)
    if not archives:
        print(f"no archived changes under {archive}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    entries = changelog_versions(root / "CHANGELOG.md")
    if entries is None:
        faults.append(
            "no CHANGELOG.md at the repository root. Every version the project ships "
            "records what changed in it"
        )
        entries = set()

    # 1. Every archived change records a version.
    for name, version in archives:
        if version is None:
            faults.append(
                f"{name} records no version. Add {VERSION_FILE} naming the version it "
                f"shipped in, so the release it belongs to is recoverable"
            )

    # 2. Every recorded version above the baseline is distinct. At or below the
    #    baseline, several changes share one release and that is recorded as the truth.
    seen: dict[tuple[int, ...], str] = {}
    for name, version in archives:
        if version is None or version <= BASELINE:
            continue
        if version in seen:
            faults.append(
                f"{name} records version {show(version)}, which {seen[version]} already "
                f"records. A version names the change that produced it"
            )
        else:
            seen[version] = name

    # 3. Every recorded version has a changelog entry, and so does the version the
    #    project currently declares.
    wanted = {version for _, version in archives if version is not None}
    wanted.add(declared)
    for version in sorted(wanted):
        if version not in entries:
            faults.append(
                f"no CHANGELOG.md heading names version {show(version)}. A shipped "
                f"version records what changed in it"
            )

    # 4. Every published runbook release is accounted for, and every runbook the repository holds
    #    can name the release it is at.
    faults.extend(published_runbook_faults(root, archive))

    # 5. Every internal dependency pins exactly the declared version.
    pins = internal_pins(root)
    faults.extend(internal_pin_faults(root, declared))

    # 6. Every published crate carries the root's license text.
    licensed = published_crates(root)
    faults.extend(license_faults(root))

    # 7. A release tag, when one is given, names the declared version.
    if arguments.tag is not None:
        faults.extend(tag_faults(arguments.tag, declared))

    if faults:
        print("release hygiene:", file=sys.stderr)
        for fault in faults:
            print(f"!!! {fault}", file=sys.stderr)
        print(
            f"\n{len(faults)} fault(s) across {len(archives)} archived change(s), "
            f"declared version {show(declared)}",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return 1

    print(
        f"  ok  {len(archives)} archived change(s) record a version, and "
        f"{len(wanted)} version(s) are in the changelog"
    )
    print(f"  ok  declared version {show(declared)} has a changelog entry")
    print(f"  ok  {len(pins)} internal dependency pin(s) name ={show(declared)}")
    print(f"  ok  {len(licensed)} published crate(s) carry the root {LICENSE_FILE}")
    if arguments.tag is not None:
        print(f"  ok  tag {arguments.tag} names the declared version")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
